Information would not be considered Confidential Information under this agreement in two main scenarios: 
1. It does not meet the definition of Confidential Information, meaning it is either public information, or non-public information that the Disclosing Party did not designate as confidential and does not reasonably need to be treated as confidential under the disclosure circumstances; 
2. It falls into one of the four explicit exclusion categories: 
   (i) It is or subsequently becomes publicly available without the Receiving Party breaching any obligation owed to the Disclosing Party; 
   (ii) It became known to the Receiving Party prior to the Disclosing Party's disclosure of such information; 
   (iii) It became known to the Receiving Party from a source other than the Disclosing Party, and that source did not breach a confidentiality obligation to the Disclosing Party; 
   (iv) It is independently developed or acquired legitimately by the Receiving Party.

